questions.explanation:
The problem tests your understanding of promises and of the fact that Promise.all does not control load.

```js
async function mapWithLimit(items, fn, limit) {
  const results = new Array(items.length);
  let next = 0;

  async function worker() {
    while (next < items.length) {
      const i = next++;              // capture the index before awaiting
      results[i] = await fn(items[i], i);
    }
  }

  const workers = Array.from(
    { length: Math.min(limit, items.length) },
    worker
  );
  await Promise.all(workers);
  return results;
}
```

The key points worth saying out loud: results are written by index (order is preserved), the index is captured before the await (otherwise you get a race), and you start no more workers than there are items. Mention separately that an error on one item rejects the whole function — and ask whether allSettled behaviour is wanted.
